// ============================================================================
// happy-number-check.epher -- does repeated digit-square summing reach 1
//
// A number is happy when iterating "replace n by the sum of the squares
// of its digits" reaches 1; unhappy numbers instead enter the cycle
// 4, 16, 37, 58, 89, 145, 42, 20, 4, which is the only other attractor
// of the map (a classical fact). The script builds the digit-square map
// with floor and mod digit extraction, checks named values, and runs the
// packed while loop (guide 1.30) behind one verdict. Checks: 19 is happy
// (19, 82, 68, 100, 1) and 4 is not.
//
// Source: the happy numbers are sequence A007770 in the OEIS; the
// classical four-cycle fact appears in Richard Guy, "Unsolved Problems
// in Number Theory", 3rd ed., section E34. Verdicts below are exact.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/happy-number-check.epher"
// In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/happy-number-check.epher
// Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
//
// There is no knob: the sample numbers are the point of the script.
// ============================================================================

// ---- the helpers -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ssq(n) adds the squares of the digits: take the last digit with mod,
// square it, and recurse on the rest after floor(n / 10).
def ssq(n) = if n == 0 then 0 else mod(n, 10) ^ 2 + ssq(floor(n / 10))
// happy(n, k) iterates the map with k steps of budget left: reaching 1
// means happy, reaching 4 means the unhappy cycle, and the k guard is a
// belt for the recursion only (every n below 10000 reaches 1 or 4 fast).
def happy(n, k) = if n == 1 then 1 == 1 else if n == 4 then 1 == 0 else if k == 0 then 1 == 0 else happy(ssq(n), k - 1)

// ---- the verdicts ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
for x in {1, 7, 19, 20, 4} do happy(x, 50)
print("19 is happy:", happy(19, 50))            // 19, 82, 68, 100, 1
print("4 is not happy:", happy(4, 50))          // it sits in the cycle
print("7 is happy:", happy(7, 50))              // 7, 49, 97, 130, 10, 1

// ---- the packed while behind the verdict -----------------------------------------------------
// v = {current value, steps}; stop on 1 (happy) or 4 (unhappy cycle).
v = {19, 0}
while v[1] != 1 and v[1] != 4 do v = {ssq(v[1]), v[2] + 1}
print("19 reaches", v[1], "after", v[2], "steps")
// ---- expected output ----
// = {true, true, true, false, false}
// = 19 is happy: true
// = 4 is not happy: false
// = 7 is happy: true
// = {19, 0}
// = 19 reaches 1 after 4 steps
